---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PaperRow from '../components/PaperRow.astro';
import publications from '../data/publications.json';

const title = 'Publications & Data — PQSensing';
const description =
  'Technical papers, audio, video, and exploratory research notes from Peterson Quantum Sensing, including an unvalidated hypothesis and experimental test plan for autonomous excitation management in superconductors.';

const papers = publications.papers;
// These existing Namecheap-hosted resources are maintained outside this repository.
// Full URLs distinguish them from local build artifacts; verify their availability on the live site.
const hosted = {
  audio: 'https://www.pqsensing.com/assets/audio/pqs-overview-2026-09.wav',
  transcript: 'https://www.pqsensing.com/assets/downloads/pqs-overview-transcript.txt',
  evidence: 'https://www.pqsensing.com/evidence.html',
  exampleCsv: 'https://www.pqsensing.com/assets/downloads/example-measurements.csv',
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <main id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-bg" style="background-image:url('/assets/bg/publications.jpg')"></div>
      <div class="page-hero-overlay"></div>
      <div class="page-hero-inner container">
        <p class="eyebrow">Publications, media &amp; data</p>
        <h1>Technical papers and research questions.</h1>
        <p class="page-hero-lede">
          Read technical papers and exploratory research notes from PQSensing.
          Each document states its scope and validation status. A proposed
          hypothesis is not an experimental result or a peer-reviewed finding.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section-pad">
      <div class="container">
        <article class="research-note" aria-labelledby="superconducting-paper-title">
          <p class="eyebrow">Exploratory research · November 15, 2026 · Version 3.0</p>
          <h2 id="superconducting-paper-title">Autonomous Excitation Management for Superconducting Stability</h2>
          <p><strong>Jon Peterson — A hypothesis and experimental test plan</strong></p>
          <p>
            Could an engineered interface or coupled device provide a preferred
            route for unwanted excitations to leave a vulnerable superconducting
            region, without a separate measurement-and-control loop? This note
            examines established precedents, physical limitations, experimental
            controls, and conditions that would count against the proposed mechanism.
          </p>
          <p class="research-status">
            <strong>Unvalidated hypothesis; not independently peer reviewed.</strong>
            No experimental results, validated simulations, or claim of novelty are
            reported. AI assistance is disclosed in the paper. This exploratory note
            is separate from the platform papers below.
          </p>
          <div class="cta-row">
            <a class="button primary" href="/research/autonomous-superconducting-stability.html">Read the full paper</a>
            <a class="button ghost" href="mailto:mroweitall@gmail.com?subject=Superconducting%20stability%20hypothesis%20feedback">Share technical feedback</a>
          </div>
          <p class="format-note">Complete, accessible web edition with all 11 references and a print-friendly layout.</p>
        </article>

        <section class="resource-panel" aria-labelledby="overview-audio-title">
          <p class="eyebrow">Current overview / September 2026</p>
          <h2 id="overview-audio-title">Listen to the PQS introduction.</h2>
          <p>A plain-language guide to the proposition, the electrical result, and the next validation step.</p>
          <audio controls preload="none" aria-label="PQS introduction">
            <source src={hosted.audio} type="audio/wav" />
            Your browser does not support audio. Use the download link below.
          </audio>
          <p class="format-note">Computer-narrated for accessibility. No autoplay.</p>
          <p><a href={hosted.audio}>Download audio</a> · <a href={hosted.transcript}>Text transcript</a></p>
          <details>
            <summary>Read the transcript</summary>
            <div class="transcript">
              <p>Precision becomes valuable when it helps someone make a decision. A stable instrument is a starting point, but holding a setting steady does not always preserve measurement quality. Peterson Quantum Sensing is developing a supervisory software or firmware layer that pays attention to a defined noise or information objective alongside an instrument's existing controls.</p>
              <p>The idea is to make small adjustments, observe their effect, and seek favorable measurement conditions. A quieter output still has to preserve the desired signal. Calibration, bias, and response speed matter.</p>
              <p>Today, PQS is at the electrical proof-of-principle stage. In one experiment with a deliberately moving optimum, active control reduced the median test objective by 3.68 decibels, about 57 percent, compared with a fixed setting after a documented tuning change. These numbers describe the same electrical objective comparison. They do not establish an improvement in optical sensitivity or customer throughput.</p>
              <p>The test used a physical electrical signal path with software-defined actuation and measurement, one programmed disturbance pattern, and separate passes in a fixed order. The next step is stronger repeated electrical testing, followed by optical validation against an independently calibrated measurement.</p>
              <p>The proposed applications include precision metrology, industrial optical inspection, and scientific interferometry. Existing stabilization, detection, and isolation tools already solve important problems. PQS's opportunity is to demonstrate additional value where a controllable quality limitation remains.</p>
              <p>For a prospective collaborator, the starting point is a focused evaluation: a clear measurement problem, an accessible adjustment, and a reference that can establish whether the final result improves. Learn more at p q sensing dot com.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </section>

        <section class="resource-panel" aria-labelledby="original-video-title">
          <p class="eyebrow">Historical concept video</p>
          <h2 id="original-video-title">The original visual explainer.</h2>
          <p>Retained from the earlier PQS site as an introduction to the concept. The current <a href={hosted.evidence}>evidence statement</a> supersedes any impression of established optical performance or product readiness.</p>
          <p><a class="button ghost" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P9Ce6NoKEwY" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open the original video on YouTube ↗</a></p>
          <p class="format-note">Opening this link connects to YouTube. Availability and playback depend on that service.</p>
        </section>

        <h2 class="platform-heading">Platform technical papers</h2>
        <p>The June 2026 papers below present concepts and proposed validation paths. They are self-published, not peer-reviewed application benchmarks. Statements about optical, atomic, or quantum systems describe research directions. For supported company performance claims, use the <a href={hosted.evidence}>September 2026 evidence page</a>.</p>
        <div class="papers-list">
          {papers.map((paper) => <PaperRow paper={paper} />)}
        </div>

        <section class="resource-panel" aria-labelledby="exploration-title">
          <h2 id="exploration-title">Interactive exploration</h2>
          <p>Explore the site's demonstrations and compare power, noise, and a normalized objective. The local CSV explorer works with measurements in your browser.</p>
          <p><a href="/index.html">Explore the demonstrations</a> · <a href={hosted.exampleCsv}>Download example CSV</a></p>
        </section>

        <div class="cta-row" style="margin-top:36px">
          <a class="button primary" href="mailto:mroweitall@gmail.com?subject=PQSensing%20publications%20inquiry">Request the full technical package</a>
          <a class="button ghost" href={hosted.evidence}>Read the evidence and its limits</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .research-note {
    border: 1px solid var(--line, rgba(96, 219, 255, 0.24));
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: clamp(20px, 4vw, 34px);
    margin: 12px 0 42px;
    background: rgba(96, 219, 255, 0.035);
  }
  .research-note h2 { max-width: 850px; line-height: 1.25; }
  .research-note p, .resource-panel p { max-width: 900px; line-height: 1.7; }
  .research-status { border-left: 3px solid currentColor; padding-left: 16px; }
  .format-note { font-size: 0.9rem; opacity: 0.85; }
  .platform-heading { margin-top: 34px; }
  .resource-panel { padding: 24px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line, rgba(96, 219, 255, 0.24)); margin: 28px 0; }
  audio { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 700px; margin: 20px 0 10px; }
  summary { cursor: pointer; }
  .transcript { padding-top: 20px; }
  .papers-list {
    margin-top: 30px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line, rgba(96, 219, 255, 0.24));
  }
</style>
